window.docContents[114].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Scalar-Theory--Large-times":{"contents":"The N-wave comparison profile has expanding negative and positive lobes,\nseparated from the zero state by shocks. A zero lobe parameter removes that\nlobe. The parameters come from extrema of the initial primitive, rather than\nfrom the separate integrals of the positive and negative parts of the\ncompactly supported datum. For Burgers's flux the comparison profile is\n\n\n\nFor a general flux the fan is centred at  and its slope and width\nare scaled by .\n\nLet  be  with  and , and let\n vanish almost everywhere outside a bounded interval, with\nboth lobe masses  and  positive. Then there is a constant  such\nthat, for the Lax–Oleinik solution ,\n\nWrite . For large , the solution vanishes outside the two\nfronts  and , up to a\nbounded distance. On an interior interval of length  the footpoint\nstays in the bounded support and the slope differs from the linear profile\n by , using a third-order expansion of the\nflux at . Two strips of bounded width near the fronts, where both\nfunctions are , contribute the leading error . For\nbounded  the bound follows from  and the finite\npropagation of the support.\n\n\n\nThe figure rescales so that the N-wave becomes a single fixed shape. The\nleft panel shows  against , and the profiles\nsettle onto the rescaled N-wave. The right panel is the  error on a\nlog scale; it follows the slope  of the theorem. The datum is placed\nso that the minimum of  sits away from the jump, which is what\nmakes the rate  sharp rather than a crude upper bound for this\nexample.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws\u0009The Scalar Theory","header":"7.5.2.8.3. Large times","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Scalar-Theory--Large-times"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Difference-Quotients___-Compactness___-and-Weak-Calculus--Rellich-Compactness":{"contents":"Sobolev embedding and Rellich compactness answer different questions.\nA continuous embedding sends every norm-bounded set to a norm-bounded set.\nA compact embedding sends every norm-bounded sequence to a sequence having a\nstrongly convergent subsequence in the target. The second conclusion is the\none needed when a weakly convergent approximation must pass through a\nnonlinear expression.\n\nIn , the sequence  converges weakly to\nzero: its pairing with each fixed  test function tends to zero.\nIt does not converge strongly, since . Moreover,\n\n\n\nconverges weakly to , whereas the square of the weak limit is zero.\nThe oscillation disappears from linear tests and survives squaring.\nStrong  convergence avoids this problem because\n\n\n\nThe unscaled sine sequence has unbounded derivative energy. Dividing it by\n gives a bounded  sequence that converges strongly to zero in\n, while its derivatives  still do not converge strongly\nin . Compactness gains convergence in a weaker norm; it does not\nmake the highest controlled derivatives converge strongly.\n\nOn a domain carrying the bounded  extension atlas used below, the\ncritical Sobolev estimate bounds each norm-bounded sequence in\n, , in , where\n\n\n\nBecause , one can extract weakly convergent subsequences in\nthe critical target. That weak convergence alone does not commute with\nproducts or nonlinear compositions. Rellich–Kondrachov gives strong\nconvergence in every strictly smaller  space,\n. It is this loss in exponent that converts boundedness into\ncompactness.\n\nEach compact embedding below concerns a concrete continuous linear map \nwhose image of the unit ball has compact closure. In the Banach spaces used\nhere, this operator statement yields the familiar sequential formulation:\nevery bounded sequence has a subsequence whose images converge strongly.\n\nFréchet–Kolmogorov first gives compactness in a lower  space:\n in the Hilbert proof and  at the endpoint. Convergence in\nmeasure, uniform integrability, and the critical  bound then give\nstrong convergence in every  with .\n\nThe Hilbert proof uses explicit approximation operators. Zero\nextension gives a globally defined  function with common bounded\nsupport. Translation by  changes it by at most\n. Finite grid averages are compact operators and\napproximate the zero-extended unit ball uniformly. A general topological\ncriterion then makes that image totally bounded.\n\nThe  endpoint follows the same plan with its own  translation\nestimate and finite-cell averaging operator, independent of the Hilbert\ncase. Once\n is known, finite measure continuously\nlowers every  source to , and the transfer theorem\nrecovers the whole range .\n\nFor , zero extension gives compactness into \nfor every finite  when  is measurable and bounded. The\nunrestricted  results below use the\nextension atlas: boundary-flattening charts,\ncutoffs, and quantitative estimates that give a bounded extension operator.\nThis lets the same translation argument work across the boundary without\nintroducing a jump. Every bounded open set with  graph boundary admits such an atlas.\n\nIn applications, compactness supplies subsequences. On an atlas domain, a\nbounded Galerkin sequence in  has a subsequence converging\nstrongly in . The same conclusion holds for a bounded\n sequence when  is measurable and bounded.\nThese estimates alone do not give convergence of the entire Galerkin scheme.\nFor evolution equations they give only spatial compactness; spacetime\ncompactness also needs time regularity and an Aubin–Lions–Simon argument.\n\n\n\nFor unrestricted spaces, the extension-atlas theorem family covers\n and , including the source endpoint  and\ntarget endpoint . The proofs use the inclusion operator and an\nexponent-restriction operator, defined first.\n\nLet , let , and let\n carry the operational bounded  atlas. The canonical\ninclusion is the continuous linear mapthat retains the order-zero coordinate and forgets the weak-derivative\ncoordinates. If  is the Sobolev graph-space value and  is its chosen\nbase representative, thenThe target  class is built from the proved Sobolev membership\n. Continuity is then obtained from the closed graph theorem:\nsource convergence gives convergence in measure through the  base\ncoordinate, target convergence gives convergence in measure through\n, and uniqueness of limits in measure identifies the graph limit.\nThus later compactness theorems all concern the same concrete operator.\n\nThe inclusion  is defined for a fixed source exponent . The\nproofs below move between source exponents, lowering  to\n or  before a compactness theorem is applied, so an\noperator that changes the exponent of every coordinate while keeping the\nsame representatives is needed. Its most general form assumes only that all\ncoordinates vanish outside a set of finite measure.\n\nLet , let , and suppose every\ncoordinate of  vanishes almost everywhere outside one\nset  of finite restricted measure. The same coordinate representatives\ndefine an element . In particular,for every symmetric multi-index  of order at most .Setwith , and write  for Lebesgue measure restricted\nto . ThenThe proof has two reusable layers. On each coordinate, the finite-measure\n estimate is applied after restricting the measure to a\nmeasurable hull of ; consequently  itself need not be measurable.\nThe coordinate estimates are assembled by the sharp finite-dimensional\n comparison, producing the factor\n.More generally, let  be bounded linear and suppose\nall coordinates of every  share the same support set . Exponent\nrestriction then composes with it to give a bounded linear mapwith operator-norm estimateThis operator form is what turns smooth cutoff localization into a map\n without rebuilding\nlinearity or continuity for that special case.\n\nOn a domain of finite measure the common support can be the whole space.\nThis special case lowers  to  and\n on bounded atlas domains.\n\nLet , let , and assume the restricted\ndomain measure of  is finite. There is a continuous linear mapthat changes only the exponent attached to each derivative coordinate.\nFor every symmetric multi-index  of order at most ,Setwith , and write  for Lebesgue measure restricted\nto . Specializing the common support in\n to the whole ambient\nspace gives the quantitative estimateand the same expression bounds the operator norm. The volume factor is the\nstandard finite-measure  estimate on each weak-derivative\ncoordinate; the  factor is inherited from the finite\n comparison for the graph-space coordinates.The construction works at every order, and Rellich uses it directly.\nExponent restriction is the identity when , composes transitively\nacross intermediate exponents, and commutes with forgetting higher Sobolev\nderivatives.\n\nThe compactness proofs themselves rest on one abstract principle: a bounded\nset that can be approximated uniformly, to any accuracy, by the images of\ncompact operators is totally bounded. The Fréchet–Kolmogorov arguments below\nproduce those approximating compact operators as finite grid averages.\n\nLet  be a real normed space and let  be bounded. Suppose\nthat for every  there is a compact continuous linear operator\n satisfyingThen  is totally bounded.\n\nChoose . Compactness of  places  inside a\ncompact set, hence inside finitely many balls of radius .\nEvery  is within  of , so the same finite set of\ncenters covers  by balls of radius .\n\nThe approximation error of a grid average is governed by how much a function\nchanges under small translations, so the analytic input is an estimate on\ntranslation defects . Translation is first set up as an isometry\nof the global  model, with the algebraic properties that the defect\ncomputations use.\n\nFor  and , the map \npreserves Lebesgue measure, so composition with it is a linear isometry of\nthe whole-space coordinate  space of the graph model:Translations form an additive action,  and\n. Translating a product translates both the\n coefficient and the field:\n. The translation defect has the\nrepresentative\n almost everywhere. Translation defects of\nzero-extended  functions and of  functions are the\nquantities estimated in the two Fréchet–Kolmogorov layers below.\n\nFor fixed , the isometry gives continuity in the function being\ntranslated. This is what passes smooth estimates to Sobolev limits in the\ndensity arguments. Integrating over shifts uses a second property:\ncontinuity in  makes the translation defect continuous on the compact\nshift box. The next theorem proves this for finite exponents; it can fail\nin .\n\nLet  and . The translation mapis continuous. In particular,  is continuous. In the\n grid argument, its square is therefore integrable on the compact\nbox of within-cell shifts.\n\nEvery  is an isometry, so it suffices to check continuity on a dense\nclass and use equicontinuity: for  close to  in ,\n. For\n, bounded continuous functions with compact support are dense,\nand for such  the last term tends to zero as  by uniform\ncontinuity and bounded support. The formal proof invokes Mathlib's joint\ncontinuity of composition with a continuously varying measure-preserving\nmap at finite exponent, applied to the continuous family\n in the compact-open topology.\n\nWe first carry out the Hilbert-space argument for zero-trace functions.\nThis is the cleanest case because zero extension introduces no artificial\nboundary derivative.\n\nLet  be measurable, let\n, and write  for its zero extension to\n. For every translation vector ,The constant is exactly one relative to the graph norm used in the\ndevelopment. The estimate is uniform on every norm-bounded subset of\n.\n\nFor restrictions of global test functions, integrate the gradient along the\nline segment from  to , then use Cauchy–Schwarz and translation\ninvariance of Lebesgue measure. Test functions are dense in\n, zero extension is isometric, and the inequality defines a\nclosed condition. Density therefore extends the smooth estimate to every\nzero-trace Sobolev value.\n\nThe translation estimate converts directly into a grid-averaging estimate:\ntwo points of a cell of width  differ by a shift of length at most\n, so the cell-average error is controlled by translation\ndefects at that scale. For bounded  the zero extension vanishes\noutside finitely many cells, which is the support hypothesis of the\nestimate, and the averaging operator then has finite rank.\n\nLet  be measurable. Fix a grid width  and a radius\n. Assume the zero extension of  vanishes almost\neverywhere outside the finite union of\ncells in the finite grid cover selected for the ball of radius .\nLet  replace the function on each retained cell by its cell\naverage. ThenFor fixed  and , the operator  has\nfinite-dimensional range and is therefore compact.\n\nThe squared cell-average error is bounded by the average of\n over pairs of points in the same\ncell. Write . Such shifts lie in the box\n, where . The translation\nestimate bounds the integrated defect byDividing by the cell volume  gives\n; taking square roots yields the stated estimate.\nOnly finitely many cells are retained, so the range is spanned by finitely\nmany cell indicators.\n\nThe grid estimate supplies exactly the hypotheses of the uniform compact\napproximation criterion, and the Hilbert case of Rellich's theorem follows in\nits global form.\n\nIf  is measurable and bounded, then zero extension followed by the\nbase-coordinate projection is a compact operator\n\nBoundedness of  places the support of every zero-extended base\ncoordinate in one fixed ball. Apply the grid estimate to the image of the\nclosed unit ball. Its error tends to zero uniformly as ,\nwhile every grid-average operator is compact. The uniform compact\napproximation criterion makes the image totally bounded; completeness of\n then gives compact closure.\n\nRestriction from  back to  is continuous, so the global\n result gives compactness on the domain. The same conclusion holds at\nevery finite exponent. Zero extension again supplies bounded support and a\nuniform translation estimate; a reduction to the  compactness criterion\nhandles the remaining exponents.\n\nLet  be measurable and bounded and let\n. The canonical base-coordinate mapis compact. Here  is the closure of smooth functions compactly\nsupported in , taken in the Sobolev graph norm. On open sets\nthis gives the usual zero-boundary space. No boundary regularity is required,\nand the theorem has no lower bound on ; in particular it includes\n. Taking  recovers the  statement above.\n\nZero extension sends the unit ball of  to a bounded\nfamily in  supported in one fixed ball. Its translations\nsatisfy a uniform bound .At , the finite-grid criterion makes this family totally bounded. For\n, apply the signed power .\nHölder's inequality gives the transformed family a uniform  translation\nbound. The same grid criterion gives compact closure in , and the\ncontinuous inverse signed power sends that compact set back into .\nThus the original zero-extended family has compact closure. Continuous\nrestriction to  proves compactness of the base-coordinate map.\nFor , the preceding Hilbert grid argument also proves this conclusion\ndirectly.\n\nFor an unrestricted  value, literal zero extension need not\nbelong to global . The operational atlas instead supplies an extension,\nafter which a cutoff creates a zero-trace value on one bounded ball.\n\nLet  and let  carry the operational\nbounded  atlas. The base-coordinate mapis compact.\n\nApply the bounded extension operator\n. Choose a smooth cutoff equal to one on\n and supported in a ball . Multiplication by the cutoff\nand restriction to  give a continuous map into . Compose this\nmap with the compact zero-extension theorem on , then restrict the\nresulting global  function back to . Extension agreement and\nthe fact that the cutoff is one on  identify this compact composite\nwith the original base-coordinate map.\n\nCompactness in one Lebesgue space suffices for the strict-subcritical transfer.\n\nLet  carry the operational bounded  atlas, let\n, and choose exponentsIfis compact, thenis compact. Taking  gives the base-exponent specialization.\n\nStart with a sequence in the closure of the image of the Sobolev unit ball.\nApproximate it by actual images . Compactness of \ngives a subsequence for which the base representatives converge in ,\nhence in measure. The critical inclusion  uniformly bounds the\nsame representatives in .Because , the critical bound makes the th powers uniformly\nintegrable. On the finite domain measure, tightness is automatic by choosing\nthe whole space. Vitali convergence upgrades convergence in measure to\nconvergence in . The original closure sequence differs from the actual\nimages by an error tending to zero, so the selected subsequence also\nconverges. Thus the closure of the image of the unit ball is sequentially\ncompact and hence compact.\n\nThe Hilbert theorem is a short consequence of the concrete\n compact operator and the transfer theorem.\n\nLet , let  carry the operational bounded  atlas, and\nlet . Thenis compact. The target endpoint  is included.\n\nAt , the general inclusion operator agrees with the compact\nbase-coordinate map. Apply strict-subcritical transfer with lower target\n and the critical  bound.\n\nLet  and let  carry the operational bounded \natlas. Then the canonical inclusionis compact.\n\nFinite measure gives the continuous coordinate-preserving map\n. Compose it with the compact\n base map. This proves compactness into\n. Compactness into every  with  is the separate\ntheorem proved below.\n\nTo reach , the development proves the endpoint translation\nestimate directly in .\n\nFor  and ,\n\nFor a smooth compactly supported function , the fundamental theorem\nof calculus along  givesIntegrate in , use translation invariance and Fubini, and bound the\n gradient by the graph norm. Global finite-exponent density approximates\nan arbitrary  value by smooth compactly supported functions.\nContinuity of translation and closedness of the inequality pass the estimate\nto the limit.\n\nThe  translation estimate takes the place of the zero-trace estimate\nof the Hilbert case. The literal zero extension of an unrestricted\n function need not be a global Sobolev function, so the\noperational atlas extension, localized in a ball containing\n, supplies a global function with bounded support instead.\n\nLet the operational  extension of  be localized\ninside one ball. The map taking a Sobolev value to the global order-zero\ncoordinate of this supported extension is compact into\n.\n\nThe supported extension operator uniformly bounds the global\n norm and places every base coordinate in one fixed ball. Finite\ngrid-cell averages have finite-dimensional range. The global translation\nestimate bounds their approximation error by a constant times the grid\nwidth, uniformly on the source unit ball. The compact-approximation\ncriterion gives total boundedness of the image and therefore compactness.\n\nRestricting to  gives the endpoint , which the Hilbert route\nabove does not reach because it needs .\n\nLet  and let  carry the operational\nbounded  atlas. Then the base-coordinate mapis compact. Stated through the general subcritical inclusion, the theorem\nacquires the additional condition , because that inclusion assumes the\nsource exponent satisfies .\n\nChoose a supported global extension inside a fixed ball. Its global\nbase-coordinate map is compact by the preceding theorem. Restriction\n is continuous, and the extension agrees\nalmost everywhere with the original function on . The resulting\ncompact composite is therefore exactly the domain base-coordinate map.\n\nExponent restriction and the transfer theorem carry the endpoint to every\nsource exponent.\n\nLet  carry the operational bounded  atlas, and let\n and . If the endpoint inclusionis compact, thenis compact.\n\nThe finite-measure restriction operator continuously lowers\n to  without changing any derivative\nrepresentative. Composing with the compact endpoint map gives compactness of\n. Apply the strict-subcritical transfer\ntheorem with lower target .\n\nCombining the endpoint theorem with the  reduction gives the\nRellich–Kondrachov theorem on operational  domains in its full\nsubcritical range.\n\nLet , let  carry the operational bounded  atlas,\nand let . Then the canonical inclusionis compact.This includes both endpoints  and .\n\nFrom  one obtains , so the endpoint inclusion is available\nthrough the general inclusion. Its compactness was proved by\nsupported extension, the global  translation estimate, and finite-cell\naveraging. Apply the  reduction theorem to obtain the desired\ntarget exponent .\n\nThe inequality  is essential. At , rescaled bumps can keep\ntheir critical norm while concentrating near one point; no strongly\nconvergent critical subsequence need exist. The library proves continuity at\nthe critical exponent and compactness below it; the computation below, which\nshows the failure at , is an informal calculation.\n\nHere is the scaling behind that counterexample. Choose an interior point\n and a nonzero smooth function  supported in the unit ball.\nFor  and sufficiently small , the functions\n\n\n\nare supported inside the domain. Changing variables gives\n\n\n\nAt , the last exponent is zero: the critical norm stays positive,\nalthough  at every . A strongly convergent\ncritical subsequence would therefore have limit zero, contradicting its\nconstant norm. Below , the exponent is positive and the same sequence\ndoes converge strongly to zero. The strict inequality in the theorem is\nexactly what makes this concentration disappear in the target norm.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Difference Quotients, Compactness, and Weak Calculus","header":"4.4.2. Rellich Compactness","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Difference-Quotients___-Compactness___-and-Weak-Calculus--Rellich-Compactness"}});